# Mobile Development Workflow

When implementing or fixing an Expo/React Native screen:

1. Read existing architecture first (`App.tsx`, `src/ui`, `src/store`, `src/theme`).
2. Follow existing components, theme tokens, and design patterns.
3. Implement the requested change with the smallest safe diff.
4. Run TypeScript checks (`npx tsc --noEmit`) and fix introduced errors.
5. Start or reload the application (`npm start` / `npm run android`, or Metro reload).
6. Use **agent-device** when available for device automation.
7. Navigate to the changed screen.
8. Visually inspect the result (`agent-device screenshot`, optional `--overlay-refs`).
9. Test important interactions (`snapshot -i`, `press` by `testID` / label / ref).
10. Inspect console/runtime errors (RN overlays, `agent-device events`, Expo terminal logs).
11. Correct any problems discovered.
12. Retest before marking work complete.

Do not consider UI work completed merely because the code compiles.

For visual UI tasks, verify the actual rendered application whenever possible.

Avoid introducing new libraries when an existing project library already solves the requirement.

Do not upgrade Expo / React Native dependencies without a specific compatibility reason.

Never expose API keys, passwords, access tokens, or secrets.

## agent-device usage

Use agent-device only for app/device automation tasks. Prefer:

`open -> snapshot -i -> act (--settle) -> re-snapshot -> verify -> close`

On this Windows machine, Android is the primary target. Open Expo Go with:

```bash
agent-device open host.exp.exponent "exp://127.0.0.1:8082" --platform android --foreground
```

Prefer stable selectors such as `id="app.mode.build"` over ephemeral `@eN` refs for durable flows.

For logs/network/RN overlays: `agent-device help debugging` / `agent-device help react-native`.

Do not probe with `--help` / `doctor` as routine prep unless setup is broken.
